<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Lloyd George</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lloyd George</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David Lloyd George was a Welsh Liberal politician and Prime Minister of the United Kingdom from 1916 to 1922. Member of Parliament for Caernarvon Boroughs for 55 years, he became Chancellor of the Exchequer in 1908 and proposed taxes on land ownership and high incomes in the People's Budget of 1909 to fund welfare reforms; its rejection by the House of Lords led to a constitutional crisis resolved by the Parliament Act 1911. His budget, the National Insurance Act 1911 and other measures helped establish the modern welfare state. He led the country through the later years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> and negotiated the establishment of the Irish Free State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george.jpg" alt="David Lloyd George" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Lloyd_George.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>David Lloyd George was born on 17 January 1863 in Chorlton-on-Medlock, Manchester, to Welsh parents, Elizabeth Lloyd and William George. His father died of pneumonia in June 1864, and his mother took the children to her native Llanystumdwy in Carnarvonshire, where they lived with her brother Richard Lloyd, a shoemaker, lay minister and strong Liberal, who became a towering influence on his nephew. He qualified as a solicitor in 1884 and set up his own practice in 1885.</p><p>He won the by-election for Caernarfon Boroughs on 10 April 1890, narrowly, and held the seat until 1945. As a radical Liberal he supported Welsh devolution, the disestablishment of the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and land reform. His fierce opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> brought him national fame, though he insisted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> should rest on freedom rather than racial arrogance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Lloyd George became President of the Board of Trade in 1905 and Chancellor of the Exchequer in 1908. His 1909 People's Budget imposed a 20 per cent tax on the unearned increase in land values, higher death duties and income tax, and a Supertax on incomes over £3,000, to pay for welfare programmes and battleships. He also helped to secure the disestablishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Anglican Church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in force from 1920.</p><p>As Minister of Munitions from 1915 he took arms production from the generals and made it an industrial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. He became Secretary of State for War in June 1916 and prime minister in December 1916, the first Welshman in the post, replacing Asquith. He created a smaller war cabinet and, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> food shortages, introduced the convoy system and rationing.</p><p>After the war he negotiated independence for the Irish Free State in 1921. In 1922 he was embroiled in a scandal over the sale of honours, and the Carlton Club meeting decided that the Conservatives should end the coalition, and he resigned. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> from 1926 to 1931, and in May 1940 his speech in the Norway Debate helped undermine Chamberlain as prime minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lloyd George died of cancer on 26 March 1945, aged 82, and was buried four days later beside the river Dwyfor at Llanystumdwy, under a boulder with no inscription. The Lloyd George Museum nearby, designed by Clough Williams-Ellis, opened in 1963. Scholars have praised his welfare reforms and his leadership to victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and criticised his presidential style and his scandals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David Lloyd George” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Manchester, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Llanystumdwy, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.48,-2.25&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Manchester on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4500 325 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of David Lloyd George"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4950 595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4950 595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.48,-2.25&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is the one thing he discovers has not changed—the world has not learned one single syllable.&#8221;</p><footer>Where Are We Going? (1923) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60262/pg60262-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Was the devil numbered amongst the slain in the last war? I have never seen his name in any casualty list.&#8221;</p><footer>Where Are We Going? (1923) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60262/pg60262-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Peace is only possible when you introduce into the attitude of nations towards each other principles which govern the demeanour of decent people in a community towards their neighbours.&#8221;</p><footer>Where Are We Going? (1923) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60262/pg60262-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You might as well have a wooden cannon; however splendidly mounted it may be, however imposing its appearance, every one knows that the moment it is fired it will burst.&#8221;</p><footer>Where Are We Going? (1923), on the League of Nations &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60262/pg60262-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
